;; Docs for samizdat/lightweight.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "lightweight.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}. A command name
;; in code is written @code|{@name}|, so that it is not run.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Commands that read lists and tables written as Markdown and Org write them.")
  (see read-list read-table)
  (example """
    @read-list{
    - this parses things
      - a list with nested lists
    - this is another post
      and this continues the list item above.
    }

    @read-table{
    #+CAPTION: What they drink
    | Name  | Cups | Of       |
    |-------+------+----------|
    | Alice |    3 | @em{tea} |
    | Bob   |   12 | coffee   |
    }
    """)
  (reference """
    The body is still samizdat: a command in it works as in any other body.
    Both commands are in @code{standard-commands} of
    @code{(samizdat commands)}.
    """))

(:doc read-list
  (summary "A command that reads a list written as Markdown and Org write one.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "An @code{ItemList} block, or nothing when the body is blank.")
  (see read-table)
  (example """
    @read-list{
    1. First
    2. Second, with a list in it:
       - a
       - b
    }
    """)
  (reference """
    The rules are the rules of Markdown, with fewer cases:

    @itemlist{
    @item{An item starts with @code{-}, @code{+} or @code{*}, or with a number and @code{.} or @code{)}, and then a space.}
    @item{The marker of the first item sets whether the list is numbered. Numbering always starts at 1.}
    @item{An item that is indented more than the marker of the item before it is in a list inside that item.}
    @item{A line of text continues the item above it, at any indentation.}
    @item{After a blank line, text that is indented more than the marker of an item is a new paragraph of that item.}
    }

    These are rejected with @code{refuse}: arguments, a call with no body, a
    body whose first line is not an item, and a line after a blank line
    that is in no item.
    """))

(:doc read-table
  (summary "A command that reads a table written as Org or Markdown write one.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "A @code{TableBlock}, or nothing when the body has no rows.")
  (see read-list)
  (example """
    @read-table[#:caption "Prices"]{
    | Item   | Price |
    |:-------|------:|
    | Tea    |  2.50 |
    | Coffee |  3.00 |
    }
    """)
  (reference """
    @itemlist{
    @item{A row is a line that starts with @code{|}. The @code{|} characters divide its cells. @code{\|} is a @code{|} in a cell. A command in a cell stays whole.}
    @item{A line of @code{-}, @code{+} and @code{|} is a rule. The rows above the first rule are the head. More rules divide the other rows into groups. A rule before the first row or after the last row has no effect.}
    @item{A delimiter row of Markdown, @code{|:---|:---:|---:|}, is a rule. Its colons set the alignment of the columns.}
    @item{A row of Org cookies, @code{| <l> | <c> | <r10> |}, sets the alignment of the columns. It is not a row of the table. Widths are ignored.}
    @item{A column with no alignment whose body cells are mostly numbers is aligned to the right.}
    @item{@code{#+CAPTION:} gives the caption. @code{#:caption "..."} gives the caption too, and replaces it. Other lines that start with @code{#+} are ignored.}
    @item{The table has as many columns as its longest row. A shorter row gets empty cells.}
    }

    These are rejected with @code{refuse}: positional arguments, a keyword
    other than @code{#:caption}, a call with no body, and a line that is not
    a row, a rule or a @code{#+} line.
    """))
